# Lines `tools/claim-sweep.py` may stop reporting.
#
# One Python regex per line, `#` for comments, blank lines ignored. Each is
# searched against `<path>: <the source line, stripped>`, so anchor with
# `^docs/foo\.md: ` to scope an entry to one page and leave it bare to
# suppress a phrase wherever it appears.
#
# An entry is a human saying "I looked, and this absolute is a code path, not
# a rate". Write the reason directly above it. Consuming a pattern spends its
# reason, so a second pattern stacked right under the first gets none of it
# -- each entry needs its own comment immediately above. An entry with no
# reason is a suppressed claim, which is the thing this tool exists to
# prevent.

# The doctrine rule states the code path itself: an unavailable optional
# dependency warns at the point of use, so a command that never touches it has
# nothing to fail on. There is no failure rate here to measure.
^docs/ar3-foundation\.md: .*never fails a command that did not need it

# The sentence carries its own proof in its own parentheses -- sqlite3 ships
# in the standard library, so FTS5 availability is a property of the
# interpreter rather than a claim about how often k7e works.
^docs/k7e-retrieval\.md: .*Always available \(stdlib sqlite3\)
